Workflow Playbook

- Intake and selection: use `az ticket list`, `az ticket search`, `az ticket get`, `az ticket get-many`, `az ticket check`, and `az ticket doctor` to find and inspect work before changing it. Search/list filters can narrow by status, dates, IDs, parent, and dependencies; request `--deps`, `--with-notes`, or `--json` only when that detail is needed.
- Creation and decomposition: `az ticket create` records tracking work; `--deferred` creates standalone backlog work; `az ticket split` creates a child and isolated execution context; `az ticket bulk-create` and `az ticket fanout` shape larger graphs. Every created ticket needs executable context, scope, and acceptance evidence.
- Ownership: `az ticket claim`, `takeover`, and `release` manage durable human/agent/orchestrator leases. Do not bypass a conflicting live owner; inspect or coordinate before takeover.
- Lifecycle: `az ticket update --status backlog|open|in_progress|in_review|closed|cancelled` applies lifecycle/review actions. Prefer `az ticket close` for accepted completion because it owns integration and cleanup. Use `az ticket delete --confirm` only for permanent deletion, and `az ticket unarchive` to restore archived work.
- Graphs: `az ticket dep add` and `az ticket dep remove` own `blocks`, `parent-child`, `related`, `discovered-from`, and `created-in` edges. `az ticket dep bulk apply`, `az ticket fanout ready`, and `az ticket fanout drift` support larger plans. Parent-child defines containment; `blocks` defines readiness.
- Evidence and resources: `az ticket record`, `az ticket events`, `az ticket context-risk`, `az observe`, and `az evidence validate` carry durable execution and closeout facts. `az ticket image` and `az ticket document` attach inspectable artifacts. Mail is for active coordination, not routine notes.
- Execution: `az session start|attach|status|diagnose|stop|restart-all|resolve-conflict` controls ticket tmux sessions; `az orchestrator-session start|attach|stop|status [--root <ticket>] [--project <project>]` controls exact-scope orchestrator sessions without treating them as tickets. `az worktree create` materializes an integration target without starting an agent. The short aliases `az start`, `az attach`, `az status`, and `az stop` map to ticket session operations.
- Integration: accepted child work normally closes through `az ticket close`. `az branch merge --source <ticket> --target <ticket|base>` and `az branch agent-merge` are low-level conflict/repair tools; merge never infers its target from the caller's worktree, and origin workflow mode refuses their direct base path. `az pr list|status|checks|open|create|merge` owns origin-mode base integration.
- Orchestration: `az orchestrate status|start|group|watch|observe|capture|prompt|message|complete-check|integrate|close-session` runs tracked graphs. `az mail send|list|watch|validate-evidence` carries worker events, and `az evidence validate` checks `worker_evidence.v1` before delivery.
- Board views: `az board view list|get|select|create|update|delete|explain` manages persistent typed projections. Use `explain` to understand first-match placement rather than inferring durable state from a column title.
- Requirements and decisions: `az spec req|link|read|pack|lint|parity` owns behavioral traceability. `az decision list|get|record|update|delete|revisit|sync|import|link` owns durable rationale and relationships.
- Learning: `az learn add|recall|show|review|promote|relate` captures evidence-backed discoveries, controls their review lifecycle, and promotes accepted guidance into rules, skills, specs, or decisions.
- Validation and development: `az gate` and `az dev gate` run configured checks. `az dev start|stop|restart|status|list` manages ticket-scoped development servers. `az githooks install|update|run|notify|hook` manages repository hook integration.
- Projects and implementations: `az project list|add|remove` manages registered repositories, and `az project scripts status` inspects project scripts. `az impl list|delete|migrate` manages implementation assignments; implementation identity never implies hierarchy.
- Runtime and diagnostics: `az daemon start|stop|restart|watch-clients`, `az operation get|list|logs|cancel`, and `az log` inspect or control daemon work. `az sync` refreshes tracker/worktree state; `az export` writes a project snapshot.
- Agent integration: `az ai install|status|uninstall|migrate|hook run` manages harness hooks and agent event reporting. `az tmux selector|install-selector|uninstall-selector` manages the tmux chooser integration.
- Configuration and discovery: `az config set` changes project configuration; `az prime` emits this session contract; `az help` and command-specific `--help` provide exact current flags.

Operational Rules Across Commands

- Put flags before positional arguments. Prefer typed `--json` output for automation and text output for direct human inspection.
- Use the daemon-backed command path rather than editing SQLite, killing tmux panes, moving worktrees, or invoking backend trackers directly. Repair commands are exceptional paths, not substitutes for normal lifecycle operations.
- Start with read commands, make one authoritative mutation, then read back the resulting ticket/session/graph projection. For destructive or terminal operations, inspect preflight output and preserve rollback evidence.
- Keep project scope explicit when operating outside the current worktree. Check command help and returned project IDs before assuming an operation is current-project or cross-project.
- Discover exact syntax at the point of use. The primer explains command ownership and workflow; `az <group> --help` remains authoritative for optional flags and rarely used variants.

Useful Discovery Commands

- `az ticket --help`, `az spec --help`, `az orchestrate --help`, `az board view --help`, `az decision --help`, `az learn --help`
- `az session status [ticket-id]`, `az board view list`, `az board view explain <ticket-id>`
- Use flags before positional arguments in documented commands.
